  "executive_narrative": "Over a 45-day operating window, KPC's order-to-cash cycle leaked KES 689.1M against KES 12.45B of expected ex-depot revenue -- 5.5% of value shipped never converts to collected cash. Annualized, that is approximately KES 5.59B at current throughput.\n\nThe largest driver is **collections shortfalls** at KES 339.6M, followed by **dispatch capture gaps** at KES 177.8M. 290 of 2826 loadings (10.3%) carry at least one reconciliation exception.\n\nLeakage concentrates geographically and commercially: Kisii depot alone accounts for KES 135.8M, and Baraka Downstream is the single largest customer-level exposure at KES 55.9M. This concentration means targeted intervention -- not a broad program -- closes most of the gap.\n\nRecommended near-term actions, in priority order: (1) Introduce automated AR aging alerts and a dunning workflow for invoices unpaid beyond terms. (2) Automate gate/dispatch confirmation capture so every loading has a matching dispatch record before it can be closed out.\n\nBusiness case (phased rollout, 35% of full network live in year one): KES 1.18B recovered against an estimated KES 75.0M year-one build cost -- payback in 0.8 months, a 15.8x first-year return. At steady state (full network live), the same recovery assumptions imply KES 3.38B per year.\n\n*Note: All KES figures are illustrative: they are computed by scaling a 45-day synthetic simulation (calibrated to plausible KPC depot volumes and ex-depot pricing, not live operational data) up to an annual run-rate. Treat them as a directional sizing of the opportunity, and re-run this same pipeline against real KPC extracts before using any number here in a board-level business case.*",
